    "single": {
      "label": "Readings",
      "readings": [
        {
          "role": "first",
          "ref": "Isaiah 50:4-9a",
          "refKey": "Isa.50.4-Isa.50.9",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "call-and-discipleship",
              "faithfulness",
              "humility-and-servanthood",
              "suffering-and-endurance"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "resolve-and-sending",
              "trust"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 50,
              "v_start": 4,
              "v_end": 4,
              "text": "Given me — This and the following passages may be in some sort understood of the prophet Isaiah, but they are far more evidently and eminently verified in Christ, and indeed seem to be meant directly of him. The tongue - All ability of speaking plainly, and convincingly, and persuasively. Weary - Burdened with the sense of his, deplorable condition. Wakeneth - Me, from time to time, and continually. To hear - He by his Divine power assists me to the practice of all his commands and my duties, with all attention and diligence."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 50,
              "v_start": 6,
              "v_end": 6,
              "text": "I gave — I patiently yielded up myself to those who smote me."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 50,
              "v_start": 8,
              "v_end": 8,
              "text": "Justifieth — God will clear up my righteousness, and shew by many and mighty signs and wonders, that I lived and died his faithful servant. Let him come - l am conscious of mine own innocency, and I know that God will give sentence for me."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 50,
              "v_start": 9,
              "v_end": 9,
              "text": "They — Mine accusers and enemies. The moth - Shall be cut off and consumed by a secret curse."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "psalm",
          "ref": "Psalm 70",
          "refKey": "Ps.70",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "deliverance",
              "suffering-and-endurance"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "lament",
              "longing-and-waiting"
            ],
            "function": [
              "lament-intercession"
            ]
          },
          "notes": []
        },
        {
          "role": "second",
          "ref": "Hebrews 12:1-3",
          "refKey": "Heb.12.1-Heb.12.3",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "call-and-discipleship",
              "saints-and-witnesses",
              "suffering-and-endurance"
            ],
            "image": [
              "cross-and-tree",
              "road-and-journey"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "exhortation"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 12,
              "v_start": 1,
              "v_end": 1,
              "text": "Wherefore, being encompassed with a cloud — A great multitude, tending upward with a holy swiftness. Of witnesses - Of the power of faith. Let us lay aside every weight - As all who run a race take care to do. Let us throw off whatever weighs us down, or damps the vigour of our Soul. And the sin which easily besetteth us - As doth the sin of our constitution, the sin of our education, the sin of our profession."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 12,
              "v_start": 2,
              "v_end": 2,
              "text": "Looking — From all other things. To Jesus - As the wounded Israelites to the brazen serpent. Our crucified Lord was prefigured by the lifting up of this; our guilt, by the stings of the fiery serpents; and our faith, by their looking up to the miraculous remedy. The author and finisher of our faith - Who begins it in us, carries it on, and perfects it. Who for the joy that was set before him - Patiently and willingly endured the cross, with all the pains annexed thereto. And is set down - Where there is fulness of joy."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 12,
              "v_start": 3,
              "v_end": 3,
              "text": "Consider — Draw the comparison and think. The Lord bore all this; and shall his servants bear nothing? Him that endured such contradiction from sinners - Such enmity and opposition of every kind Lest ye be weary - Dull and languid, and so actually faint in your course."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "gospel",
          "ref": "John 13:21-32",
          "refKey": "John.13.21-John.13.32",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "passion-and-cross",
              "sin-and-fall"
            ],
            "image": [
              "bread",
              "darkness-and-night"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "lament"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 13,
              "v_start": 21,
              "v_end": 21,
              "text": "One of you — The speaking thus indefinitely at first was profitable to them all."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 13,
              "v_start": 23,
              "v_end": 23,
              "text": "There was lying in the bosom of Jesus -- that is, sitting next to him at table. This phrase only expresses the then customary posture at meals, where the guests all leaned sidewise on couches. And each was said to lie in the bosom of him who was placed next above him. One of the disciples whom Jesus loved — St. John avoids with great care the expressly naming himself. Perhaps our Lord now gave him the first proof of his peculiar love, by disclosing this secret to him."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 13,
              "v_start": 24,
              "v_end": 24,
              "text": "Simon Peter — Behind Jesus, who lay between them."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 13,
              "v_start": 25,
              "v_end": 25,
              "text": "Leaning down, and so asking him privately."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 13,
              "v_start": 26,
              "v_end": 26,
              "text": "Jesus answered — In his ear. So careful was he not to offend (if it had been possible) even Judas himself. The sop - Which he took up while he was speaking. He giveth it to Judas - And probably the other disciples thought Judas peculiarly happy! But when even this instance of our Lord's tenderness could not move him, then Satan took full possession."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 13,
              "v_start": 27,
              "v_end": 27,
              "text": "What thou doest, do quickly — This is not a permission, much less a command. It is only as if he had said, If thou art determined to do it, why dost thou delay? Hereby showing Judas, that he could not be hid, and expressing his own readiness to suffer."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 13,
              "v_start": 28,
              "v_end": 28,
              "text": "None knew why he said this — Save John and Judas."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 13,
              "v_start": 30,
              "v_end": 30,
              "text": "He went out — To the chief priests. But he returned afterward, and was with them when they ate the passover, Matt. xxvi, 20, though not at the Lord's Supper."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 13,
              "v_start": 31,
              "v_end": 31,
              "text": "Jesus saith — Namely, the next day; on Thursday, in the morning. Here the scene, as it were, is opened, for the discourse which is continued in the following chapters. Now - While I speak this, the Son of man is glorified - Being fully entered into his glorious work of redemption. This evidently relates to the glory which belongs to his suffering in so holy and victorious a manner."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
